---
import ArticleLayout from '../../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import ArticleFaq from '../../../components/ArticleFaq.astro';
import CTASection from '../../../components/CTASection.astro';
import { MANUFACTURER, CERTIFICATIONS, TRADE_TERMS, RELATIONSHIP } from '../../../data/site';

const title = 'OEM Battery Supplier Checklist | Dingwei Battery';
const description = 'A supplier evaluation checklist for OEM battery sourcing: specification, certifications, factory capability, sampling, terms and repeatability.';
const articleTitle = 'OEM battery supplier checklist';

const faqs = [
  { q: 'What red flag should make me stop immediately?', a: 'A quote given before the standard family and CCA basis are fixed. If a supplier prices a &ldquo;72 Ah battery&rdquo; without asking which standard, they are guessing — and so is the price.' },
  { q: 'Is a checklist enough to protect me?', a: 'A checklist is a filter, not a guarantee. It catches weak suppliers but does not replace testing a sample and confirming change control in the contract.' },
  { q: 'What separates a dependable supplier from a one-sample wonder?', a: 'Repeatability. Ask how they keep the tenth container identical to the approved sample — a supplier that cannot describe its change control is a risk.' },
];
---
<ArticleLayout title={title} description={description} articleTitle={articleTitle} articleSection="Procurement" dateModified="2026-09-03" faqs={faqs}>
  <section class="section container prose">
    <span class="tag">Knowledge</span>
    <h1>OEM battery supplier checklist</h1>
    <p class="byline">Published by Dingwei Battery · Last reviewed 2026-09-01 · Source: Chengguang Energy technical documentation</p>
    <p class="lead">Run a supplier through these checks before you commit to an OEM battery order.</p>

    <div class="takeaways">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> The red flag that should stop you immediately is a quote given before the standard family and CCA basis are fixed — a supplier that prices a &ldquo;72 Ah battery&rdquo; without asking which standard is guessing, and so is the price. A checklist is a filter, not a guarantee.
    </div>

    <h2>Specification</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Standard family confirmed: JIS, DIN, BCI, AS or SA.</li>
      <li>Voltage, capacity (Ah) and CCA agreed in writing.</li>
      <li>Case size, terminal type and polarity defined.</li>
      <li>Reference model or drawing attached to the order.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Certifications</h2>
    <p>Request current certificates rather than marketing claims. {MANUFACTURER.name} holds {CERTIFICATIONS.join(', ')}.</p>

    <h2>Factory capability</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Factory area and number of production lines.</li>
      <li>Daily output versus your annual volume.</li>
      <li>Export experience and destination markets.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>{MANUFACTURER.name} operates a {MANUFACTURER.factoryArea} plant with {MANUFACTURER.productionLines} and exports to {MANUFACTURER.exportMarkets}.</p>

    <h2>Commercial terms</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>MOQ: {TRADE_TERMS.moq}.</li>
      <li>Lead time: {TRADE_TERMS.leadTime}.</li>
      <li>Payment: {TRADE_TERMS.payment}.</li>
      <li>Incoterms: {TRADE_TERMS.incoterms}.</li>
      <li>Warranty: {TRADE_TERMS.warranty}.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Repeatability</h2>
    <p>Confirm how the manufacturer keeps subsequent orders identical to the approved sample — label, packaging, specification and quality.</p>
    <p><a href="/knowledge/how-choose-oem-battery-manufacturer/">How to choose a manufacturer &rarr;</a> · <a href="/oem/">Dingwei OEM programs &rarr;</a></p>

    <h2>Red flags</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>No current certificates when you ask for them.</li>
      <li>Quoting before the standard family and CCA basis are fixed.</li>
      <li>No sample available from a recent production run.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>If any of these appear, slow the process down and re-check the <a href="/knowledge/how-choose-oem-battery-manufacturer/">five-point decision framework</a> before placing volume.</p>
    <p>{RELATIONSHIP}</p>

    <h2>Check the quote, not just the price</h2>
    <p>A quote is a six-part scope. If any part is missing, the number is not comparable:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Tooling costs</strong> — one-time or phased, and refundable or not.</li>
      <li><strong>MOQ structure</strong> — prototype / pilot / production clearly separated.</li>
      <li><strong>Test report scope</strong> — transport, safety and performance reports listed.</li>
      <li><strong>Warranty terms</strong> — duration, exclusions and failure handling.</li>
      <li><strong>Engineering support</strong> — included or separately charged.</li>
      <li><strong>Change control</strong> — a formal ECO process, not a verbal agreement.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Use the same six fields when you issue the <a href="/knowledge/oem-battery-rfq-guide/">OEM battery RFQ</a> so suppliers are forced to quote on scope, not on a bare unit number.</p>
  </section>

  <ArticleFaq faqs={faqs} />

  <CTASection title="Put Dingwei Battery through your checklist" />
</ArticleLayout>
